# Line endings
#
# core.autocrlf is false and this repo has always stored bytes exactly as written.
# Every .cs file, and every Visual Studio project file, is CRLF *in the committed
# blob itself* - not LF that git converts on checkout.
#
# That is why nothing here says `text eol=crlf`. It reads as "these files are CRLF"
# but it means "store LF in the blob and convert on checkout", which is the opposite
# of what this history contains. Setting it made git run every file through the
# clean filter before diffing, so all 482 lines of Board.cs and all 172 lines of
# Scrolls.csproj registered as changed when one line had been added. Files nobody
# had touched, Screen.cs and AssemblyInfo.cs among them, would have been rewritten
# by the next `git add` as well.
#
# `-text` is the declaration that matches the bytes: git converts nothing, in either
# direction, and a diff shows only what actually changed.
#
# The cost is that this cannot *enforce* CRLF the way the previous version intended.
# A tool that rewrites a source file with bare LF still produces a whole-file diff.
# Guarding against that stays a manual convention, documented in CLAUDE.md, and the
# check is: any 0x0A not preceded by 0x0D.
#
# Switching to enforcement later means `git add --renormalize .` and one commit that
# rewrites every blob in the tree. That is a deliberate decision, not a side effect
# of editing this file.

# Convert nothing by default.
* -text

# Sources and project files: CRLF on disk and CRLF in the blob.
#
# Board.cs and Screen.cs also carry non-ASCII box-drawing glyphs and must stay UTF-8
# with BOM. Deliberately NOT declared with working-tree-encoding: that attribute is
# for non-UTF-8 working trees, and pointing it at UTF-8 here would put git in charge
# of a byte sequence the C# compiler depends on. The BOM stays a manual convention.
*.cs        -text
*.csproj    -text
*.sln       -text
*.config    -text
*.settings  -text
*.xsd       -text
*.xsc       -text
*.xss       -text
*.user      -text
*.ps1       -text

# Documentation and dotfiles are LF, which is how they are already committed.
*.md           -text
.gitignore     -text
.gitattributes -text

# Binary. SQL CE databases in particular must never be line-ending converted;
# they are the only copies of the card data.
*.sdf  binary
*.exe  binary
*.dll  binary
*.pdb  binary
*.cache binary
*.vsidx binary
*.suo  binary

# CSV card exports are text but tools disagree on endings; leave them alone.
*.csv  -text
